# Relationship.is_type_definition — fills SCIP's "go to type definition"
# relationship (Relationship.is_type_definition, field 4), which upstream
# leaves unset on 100% of relationships. A syntactic type resolver
# (`tryResolveDeclaredTypeDecl` in indexer/Indexer.cc, same style as the kind
# patch's `classifySymbolKind`) records, for a field / variable, a
# {symbol: <type>, is_type_definition: true} entry on the field's / variable's
# OWN SymbolInformation — the source of the edge is the field/variable itself
# and the destination is its declared type (what builder-style consumers read
# out of `sym_info.relationships`). The resolution policy is the exact one
# already established by trySaveTypeReference: a typedef/alias type resolves
# to the typedef declaration, a tag type to the tag declaration, and a
# template specialization to the primary templated declaration (so a field of
# std::vector<Foo> gets a single edge to std::vector; template arguments are
# not followed). Pointer, reference and cv-qualifier wrappers are stripped
# first (e.g. `const Foo&` resolves to Foo). Builtin types, `auto` without a
# resolvable named declaration, dependent types, and any other shape without
# a compiler-resolved SCIP symbol emit no relationship at all — never a
# partial or guessed one (same "only exact, compiler-traced facts" philosophy
# as the other patches). `declaredType` can be a null QualType on malformed /
# error-recovery AST nodes (e.g. broken templates); tryResolveDeclaredTypeDecl
# guards for that before dereferencing it.
#
# Scope (accepted v1 limitation, mirroring the kind patch's): only
# declarations which get a SymbolInformation emitted today — fields
# (saveFieldDecl) and file-scope variables / static data members (saveVarDecl's
# SymbolInformation branch). Local variables and parameters get
# saveDefinition(std::nullopt, ...) and have nowhere to attach a relationship;
# this patch deliberately does NOT extend SymbolInformation emission to them.
#
# Mechanism note (why the relationship is not attached directly to the
# symbolInfo local in saveFieldDecl/saveVarDecl): those functions' bodies are
# bracketed by lines other patches in this directory create or rewrite — the
# saveDefinition(...) calls following the insertion point are rewritten by
# enclosing_range (upstream's one-line call becomes a two-line one), so a
# hunk anchored there would only apply in orderings where enclosing_range ran
# first. Instead the relationship is recorded as a standalone
# SymbolInformation in a new PartialDocument::extraSymbolInfos list — kept out
# of the per-symbol map so it can never displace the declaration's primary
# SymbolInformation. TuIndexer::emitDocumentOccurrencesAndSymbols folds each
# extra's relationships into the matching primary SymbolInformation (looked up
# in doc.symbolInfos by symbol name) *before* draining that map, and emits
# only the (now relationship-bearing) primary — never the extra as a second,
# separate SymbolInformation; an extra with no matching primary (not expected
# to happen: the field/var's own saveFieldDecl/saveVarDecl call always
# populates doc.symbolInfos before this method runs) is emitted standalone as
# a fallback rather than silently dropped. This union has to happen directly
# in that method: DocumentBuilder::merge (upstream's cross-TU dedup pass)
# only runs for documents indexed from more than one TU — the vast majority
# of documents (every single-TU .cpp file, and any header included from just
# one TU) never reach it, so relying on it to fold the extra into the primary
# would leave those documents with two separate SymbolInformation entries for
# the same symbol: a real duplicate-record bug in the emitted SCIP, not just
# a downstream-tolerant quirk. No ScipExtras changes are needed: upstream's
# RelationshipExt hash already combines is_type_definition. NOT (yet) an
# upstream PR.
#
# Applicability: generated with minimal diff context (`-U1`) so it applies
# cleanly in any order relative to the other five patches in this directory
# (or completely alone) — every hunk is anchored on lines no other patch
# creates or rewrites. Exception: the emitDocumentOccurrencesAndSymbols hunk
# that appends the (unmerged-fallback) extras after the main symbol-emission
# loop carries -U2 context. At -U1 its context would be just `}));` + `}` —
# that exact pair also occurs at the end of
# MacroIndexer::emitDocumentOccurrencesAndSymbols, so git apply could match a
# closer-but-wrong brace pair (the same failure mode the forward-definition
# patch's header documents). The richer context anchors on the unique
# *scipDocument.add_symbols() = std::move(symInfo); line instead. The hunk
# that inserts the merge pass itself (right before the `extractTransform(`
# call) needs no such widening even at -U1: its trailing context — a bare
# `extractTransform(` with no `scip_clang::` qualifier, immediately preceded
# by a `}` — occurs nowhere else in the file (every other call site is
# qualified as `scip_clang::extractTransform(`). See
# scip-clang-patches/README.md's "Independence" section.
